# SVG — format contract

`context/instructions/vector-svg.md` is the markup contract for the SVG format.
Read it together with `context/instructions/vector.md` (the authoring doctrine:
concept first, then the render-inspect-critique-fix loop) and
`context/instructions/vector-tools.md` (the render tooling) whenever the vector
artefact is SVG. Where this file and the project's existing SVG convention
disagree, the project's convention wins.

## Skeleton

```svg
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512" role="img" aria-labelledby="t d">
  <title id="t">Short title</title>
  <desc id="d">Description for a reader who cannot see it.</desc>
  <!-- content -->
</svg>
```

## Scaling

- Always declare a `viewBox`; avoid fixed root `width`/`height` so the SVG adapts
  to its container.
- Choose a simple coordinate space (`24×24` for icons, `512×512` or `1000×1000`
  for illustrations) and use integer or low-precision coordinates.
- Declare `xmlns` on standalone files.
- Documented exception: when physical size is the point (print), a fixed
  `width`/`height` is legitimate.

## Structure and ids

- **Semantic, not positional, ids**: `logo-group`, `headline-text`,
  `decorative-stripe-1`. Edit by id; never regenerate the whole file for a small
  fix.
- Group with `<g>` and put shared attributes on the group, not on every element.
- Reuse what repeats (shapes, gradients, patterns) through `<defs>`, `<symbol>`
  and `<use>`.

## Drawing rules

- Prefer primitives (`rect`, `circle`, `ellipse`, `line`, `polygon`) to `path`
  when they suffice; in a `path`, use the fewest nodes that keep the curve smooth,
  keep absolute/relative commands consistent, and close shapes with `Z`.
- Constant stroke width per family; consistent `stroke-linecap`/`stroke-linejoin`;
  `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` when a stroke must not scale.
- Colour: a limited, defined palette; `currentColor` for monochrome icons; CSS
  variables with a fallback for a themed inline SVG; gradients only when they serve
  a purpose, defined once in `<defs>` and reused; check contrast on light and dark
  backgrounds.
- Text: avoid `<text>` with external fonts (not guaranteed). If text is part of
  the drawing, convert it to paths; if it must stay text, give a system-font
  fallback list.
- Effects: filters and masks cost rendering and support varies — use them
  sparingly and check the result. Never embed a base64 raster in an SVG that must
  stay vector.

## Accessibility

- Informative image: `role="img"` plus `<title>` and `<desc>`.
- Decorative image: `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Never carry meaning by colour alone.

## Security (trust boundary)

- No `<script>`, no event handlers (`onclick`, …), no `<foreignObject>` unless
  justified, no references to external resources (remote `href`, `@import`).
- Sanitise any third-party or user-supplied SVG before inlining it into a page.

## Optimisation

- Run the file through an optimiser (for example SVGO), preserving the `viewBox`
  and the ids that are referenced.
- Strip metadata, comments, editor attributes (Inkscape/Illustrator), empty groups
  and unused defs; reduce numeric precision to one or two decimals.
- Keep a readable source (groups + ids) and a separate optimised production copy.
- Verify well-formed XML, and render in a browser **and** one independent
  renderer (see `context/instructions/vector-tools.md` for the profiled tools and
  the render recipe).

## Document management

- Place an asset where the project's corpus keeps its assets; if there is no
  existing convention, an `assets/` directory beside the documents that use it is
  the default. Name files semantically (`brand-mark.svg`, not `final-v2.svg`).
- Reference an asset by a path relative to the document that uses it, or through
  the project's existing image/frontmatter convention — do not invent a new one.
- Review an asset at the sizes it is actually shown at (16/24/64 px for icons)
  before publishing it.

## Size budgets (defaults, not laws)

- Icon: under ~2 KB.
- Simple illustration: under ~20–30 KB.
- These are review signals with a reason (transferred bytes, parse cost); a
  complex illustration legitimately exceeds them. Override knowingly by recording
  the project's own budget.

## Self-check (pass/fail, no score)

State each rule as its own pass or fail — never collapse them into a score,
because a score lets a file with a missing `viewBox` pass.

- `viewBox` present; no unnecessary fixed root size.
- Palette defined and respected; themes considered where relevant.
- Render inspected visually, including at reduced size.
- Style, strokes and detail level consistent (see `vector.md`).
- Groups carry clear ids; repeated elements are reused.
- `<title>`/`<desc>` or `aria-hidden` set.
- No script, event handler, external reference or external font.
- Optimised and still well-formed after optimisation.
